      $ dhcpcd --help | grep -i metric
    [*] Disable ASUS QoS: Turn off Quality of Service (QoS) or Traffic Analyzer
      settings in the ASUSWRT firmware dashboard, as the router's older CPU can
      bottleneck local high-speed transfers when these are active.
    [!] WTF: Bad/damaged/marginal cable
      * Bad/damaged/marginal cable — most common cause by far. Even a cable that "looks fine" can have a kinked or crushed section, a bad crimp on the connector, or be borderline for Cat5e/Cat6 spec.
      * Cable run too long or routed alongside power lines/other interference sources (fluorescent ballasts, motors, etc.)
      * Bad port on either end (NIC or router LAN port) — connector wear, oxidation, etc.
      * Wrong/degraded category cable for sustained Gigabit — Cat5 can technically link at 1000Mb/s but is out-of-spec and prone to exactly this under load


